John H. wrote:
> Martin Read wrote:
> 
>>The problem is that Nethack is so complex that identifying all the
>>places where things might need to be changed to adjust the game's
>>difficulty is probably beyond reasonable feasibility.
> 
> 
> I disagree with this statement.  To believe this is to believe that
> Nethack can exist in no better form than in wihch it exists now.  It's
> great, but it is not perfect.
> 
> But I don't think ensuring players always know Identify (which could be
> easier done by simply starting all players off with one identify
> scroll) is a good idea.  The process of discovering that first scroll
> is too important a part of the game.
> 

Deeper dungeon levels are more difficult. The same adjustments as
achieve this could be made for the game as a whole.

As I understand it, currently the game uses dungeon level to determine 
which special rooms and traps to generate, the average of player and 
dungeon level to generate random monsters on the way down, and the 
maximum dungeon level to genrate monsters once you've got the amulet.

On the insanely hard setting, the game could add 10 to each of
these values, so you can get polymorph traps on level 1.

On the ridiculously easy setting, it would subtract 10, but with
a minimum of 1, so a first level player could get down to level 20
before the effective dungeon level rose above one.

Other settings would have intermediate modifiers. The dungeon branch
locations would be unaffected by all this.

No doubt there would be complications, but doing this alone should
come close enough to adjustable difficulty.


